# Quality gate configuration for this repository.
#
# One mode per gate: off, observe, warn, enforce. Every mode below enforce needs
# a date (<gate>_until) and a reason (<gate>_why) — an undated exception is not
# an exception, it is the new normal. Assessed 2026-08-24 by running each tool
# against the tree; the modes below follow what was measured, not a template.

# --- gates -------------------------------------------------------------------

# gitleaks over the working tree and the commit range: no findings.
secrets=enforce

# gofmt -l reports nothing.
format=enforce

# go vet ./... is clean. Pinned to go vet rather than left to the adapter, which
# prefers staticcheck when it happens to be installed — the gate has to mean the
# same thing on every machine. Introducing staticcheck is a separate decision:
# it has a wider ruleset and its findings here are unmeasured.
lint=enforce
lint_cmd="go vet ./..."

# In Go the compiler is the type checker, so this is the build command. Kept as
# its own gate because the report should say which of the two failed.
typecheck=enforce

# go build ./... from a clean checkout, no local step.
build=enforce

# Unit tests for the pure logic in pkg/api, pkg/db and pkg/config, plus
# integration tests for pkg/db against a real MongoDB. Added 2026-08-24, green.
# -race is on because main runs two servers and a signal handler concurrently.
#
# The integration tests skip themselves when no database is reachable, which is
# the wanted convenience locally and a blind spot in a pipeline — so CI sets
# REQUIRE_MONGO=1, which turns the skip into a failure. A green local run of this
# gate therefore does not mean the integration tests ran; point MONGO_TEST_URL at
# a database to include them. MONGO_TEST_URL, not MONGO_URL: the service's own
# variable must not be able to aim the tests at a deployment.
test=enforce
test_cmd="go test -race ./..."

# Coverage of the changed lines against the repository average, never an absolute
# percentage: a low-coverage repository can meet that on the first day and the
# average only rises from there. The adapter has no default for this gate at all,
# and there is no off-the-shelf command either — the criterion needs the diff and
# the coverage profile read together. tools/coveragegate runs the suite with
# -covermode=set, resolves the base ref the same way the runner does, and compares
# the two ratios by cross multiplication so that "exactly at the average" passes
# instead of depending on the last bit of a division.
#
# Measured 2026-08-24 on the branch that introduces it: changed lines 60.83%
# (424/697) against a repository average of 57.13% (609/1066). Two caveats worth
# knowing before this blocks something:
#
#   - The ratio is computed over everything the branch adds, not over one commit,
#     so a green run here does not predict the next one. Short-lived branches are
#     the mitigation.
#   - That 57% average includes tools/vulngate and tools/coveragegate, which arrived
#     with their own tests and lifted it. The service code is not evenly covered
#     behind that figure: main.go, pkg/api/handler.go and pkg/service are at zero.
#     A change confined to those has to bring tests to clear the average — which is
#     the gate working, but it is a higher bar than the service code alone implies.
#
# It runs the full suite, so it needs the same MongoDB environment as the test
# gate; without it the average measures 43% instead of 57% because the integration
# tests skip. That means the suite runs twice per job, once here and once for the
# test gate — measured 2026-08-24 at 3.8 s and 4.5 s, against a job whose cost is
# dominated by downloads. Teaching the tool to reuse a profile would save those
# four seconds and make this copy diverge from the one it came from, which is the
# worse trade.
coverage_delta=enforce
coverage_delta_cmd="go run ./tools/coveragegate"

# go-licenses over all packages: 26 Apache-2.0, 25 MIT, 15 BSD-3-Clause, nothing
# else. The allow list is explicit rather than type-based, so a new license shows
# up as a decision instead of being sorted into a category.
#
# If this reports "Package errors does not have module info" for a hundred standard
# library packages, the go-licenses binary was built by a different Go than the one
# this module runs: it recognises the standard library by comparing against the
# GOROOT baked in at its own build time. The toolchain directive in go.mod makes
# that the normal case on a machine whose own Go is older, because the module then
# runs a toolchain unpacked into the module cache. The fix is one command, run from
# inside this repository so the module's toolchain builds it:
#
#     go install github.com/google/go-licenses@v1.6.0
#
# CI installs it in the same job as everything else, so the two always match there.
deps_license=enforce
deps_license_cmd="go-licenses check ./... --allowed_licenses=MIT,Apache-2.0,BSD-3-Clause,BSD-2-Clause,BSD-2-Clause-FreeBSD,ISC"

# Neither the adapter default nor plain govulncheck measures the criterion: both
# exit non-zero on any called vulnerability, including a transitive one with no
# fix available, which the catalog names as the explicit non-goal. tools/vulngate
# keeps the called findings and fails only on those that have a fix AND sit in a
# direct requirement or the Go distribution, printing the rest either way so a
# green run still names what is known and unfixable.
#
# Measured 2026-08-25 against the dependency set on main: 3 called findings, 0
# blocking. All three are transitive — x/text and grpc have fixes but are not
# direct requirements, golang-jwt/jwt v3 has no fix at all because that major line
# is end of life. The one finding that did qualify when this was first assessed,
# GO-2026-5327 in mongo-driver, is closed by the update to v2.6.0 already on main.
#
# Note this gate is toolchain-sensitive by design: a Go distribution with a known
# fixable vulnerability blocks, because its standard library ends up in the built
# binary. Measured 2026-08-24, go1.26.3 fails here with 8 such findings while
# go1.25.14 has none — the fix is to update the local Go, not to change the gate.
# The released image is unaffected either way: the Dockerfile builds on golang:1.25,
# which resolves to the newest patch at build time.
vulns=enforce
vulns_cmd="go run ./tools/vulngate"

# --- path intensity ----------------------------------------------------------

# Persistence, and the single place where the caller's identity is decided. The
# list stays this narrow on purpose: a critical path escalates every observe and
# warn gate to enforce for that run, so naming a whole layer here — the entire
# pkg/api package, say — would make the escalation apply to nearly every change
# and turn deadlines into decoration. There is nothing below enforce to escalate
# today; the entry is what keeps that true if something is ever deferred.
CRITICAL_PATHS='pkg/db/* pkg/api/api.go'

# Documentation and the license text cannot break the service. docs/ is not in
# this list on purpose: docs/swagger.json is generated from the handler comments
# and a difference there is a real signal, not noise.
RELAXED_PATHS='*.md LICENSE'
